WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.403 --> 00:00:04.143
(upbeat orchestral music)

00:00:04.143 --> 00:00:07.143
(audience applauds)

00:00:17.629 --> 00:00:20.546
(audience cheers)

00:00:48.241 --> 00:00:49.770
- Thank you everyone.

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:50.881
I mean, it's just wonderful

00:00:50.881 --> 00:00:54.120
to see this room and see all of you here.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:58.500
And so many of you have been
part of the Institute's work,

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:02.400
but I, I actually wanna start
by thanking Ryan Coogler

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:04.290
and Chloe Zhao.

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:06.218
It was such, yes.

00:01:06.218 --> 00:01:09.301
(audience applauds)

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:13.500
It has been such a
privilege to support both

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:16.440
of you on your
unforgettable first features

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:19.290
means the world to me.

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:24.290
So thank you for being here
with your full heart for us.

00:01:25.474 --> 00:01:29.096
For over four decades, the Sundance Labs,

00:01:29.096 --> 00:01:32.696
the Festival, have
provided unwavering support

00:01:32.696 --> 00:01:35.733
for a next generation of artists.

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:38.430
And at the same time, we've worked

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:41.790
with many others in this
room, so many in this room

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:43.151
to build an industry

00:01:43.151 --> 00:01:47.997
and an audience for
groundbreaking independent film.

00:01:47.997 --> 00:01:51.080
(audience applauds)

00:01:56.018 --> 00:01:59.610
I just wanna say you are
all change makers here.

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:02.700
So I'm gonna talk about
my path to Sundance,

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:04.765
which began in Boston.

00:02:04.765 --> 00:02:09.030
And now I'm gonna take you back to 1981

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:13.230
and very unexpectedly, a
close friend, Lynn Benjamin,

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:17.190
asked me a question that
would change my life.

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:19.740
Would I consider coming to Sundance, Utah

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:22.440
for the first month long filmmaker's Lab

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:25.038
that Robert Redford was starting up?

00:02:25.038 --> 00:02:28.310
Lynn also introduced me to my husband,

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:31.880
but that's another story here.

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:35.737
I arrived at Sundance on
a beautiful blue sky day.

00:02:35.737 --> 00:02:39.438
I still remember the whispering
music of the quaking Aspens

00:02:39.438 --> 00:02:43.801
and the sight of Majestic
Mount Timpanogos.

00:02:43.801 --> 00:02:46.015
In that magical setting,

00:02:46.015 --> 00:02:48.937
Redford laid out his radical vision,

00:02:48.937 --> 00:02:52.980
well-established screenwriters, directors,

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:56.760
actors would mentor bold
independent filmmakers

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:59.160
early in their careers.

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:00.930
Redford wanted to build a community

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:04.057
that honored creative
freedom, risk-taking,

00:03:04.057 --> 00:03:07.740
a culture of generosity,
which meant the world to me,

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:12.330
and storytellers from
underrepresented communities.

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:15.993
For me, this was love at first sight.

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:20.130
My journey might have ended at
that first visit to Sundance,

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:24.000
but I wanted more and I asked
for five minutes with Bob

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.420
before leaving the mountain,
and I got those five minutes.

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:30.660
So with all the confidence
that I could muster,

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:33.604
I told him he needed
me to open an LA office

00:03:33.604 --> 00:03:38.011
and help him fulfill the
vision that he had created.

00:03:38.011 --> 00:03:40.299
He looked at me for a moment

00:03:40.299 --> 00:03:44.921
and then he said, "Yeah, sure,
call me when you get there."

00:03:44.921 --> 00:03:46.560
(laughter)

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:48.921
And right then and
there, I learned quickly

00:03:48.921 --> 00:03:53.678
that when somebody says,
"yes," stop talking.

00:03:53.678 --> 00:03:56.500
(laughter)

00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:58.590
(audience applauds)

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:00.060
I returned to Boston

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:03.284
and thought that maybe I had
imagined the conversation.

00:04:03.284 --> 00:04:06.840
Nervously, I called his
assistant the next day,

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:08.760
told her what Bob had said.

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:11.460
She laughed and said she would check

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:12.860
with him and let me know.

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:17.860
The next day I got the call
that forever changed my life.

00:04:19.710 --> 00:04:22.782
So today, with immense
gratitude, I'm honored

00:04:22.782 --> 00:04:26.580
to accept the Jean
Hersholt Humanitarian Award

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:29.310
in recognition of my
lifelong work supporting

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:31.980
independent visionary filmmakers,

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:34.964
as a founding director of
the Sundance Institute.

00:04:34.964 --> 00:04:39.047
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:04:47.822 --> 00:04:49.767
I wanna congratulate the
extraordinary artists

00:04:49.767 --> 00:04:52.270
who are also being honored here tonight.

00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:54.569
I mean, what an amazing group.

00:04:54.569 --> 00:04:58.484
Angela Bassett, Mel
Brooks, Carol Littleton.

00:04:58.484 --> 00:05:01.230
So moved by all of you.

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:05.545
And my huge thanks to
Janet Yang, Bill Kramer

00:05:05.545 --> 00:05:08.910
of the Academy, Marina Zenovich,

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:10.464
for making such a moving short

00:05:10.464 --> 00:05:14.283
and for all the governors
for this incredible honor.

00:05:15.393 --> 00:05:17.400
The journey that brought me to this moment

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:18.750
was not taken alone,

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:22.200
and it was shared every
step and every mile

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:25.524
with my brilliant colleagues
over four decades.

00:05:25.524 --> 00:05:29.385
I wanna acknowledge
Joana Vicente, our CEO,

00:05:29.385 --> 00:05:32.096
Ebs Burnough, chair of our board.

00:05:32.096 --> 00:05:34.980
llyse McKimmie, the deputy director

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:37.337
of our feature film program,

00:05:37.337 --> 00:05:41.115
Tammy Rosen, our head
of communications, along

00:05:41.115 --> 00:05:45.960
with our extraordinary board
members, funders, partners,

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:48.930
actors, crews, everyone who's been part

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:53.090
of the credible community that
was built and the journey.

00:05:53.090 --> 00:05:56.250
I also wanna extend my deepest gratitude

00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:59.100
to the filmmakers I've had
the privilege of working with.

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:03.480
And I really underline it is
a privilege whose storytelling

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:05.355
has ignited profound change

00:06:05.355 --> 00:06:08.460
through their creative
brilliance and courage

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:10.830
to spark vital conversations.

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:14.568
Their stories have become a
powerful force for empathy,

00:06:14.568 --> 00:06:19.568
understanding, advocacy,
and yes, entertainment.

00:06:21.027 --> 00:06:24.110
(audience applauds)

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:29.850
And over the past three years,

00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:32.760
the filmmakers who've been
celebrated with Academy Awards

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:36.120
for Best Picture have
all been supported early

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:39.733
in their careers by the
Sundance Labs and Festival.

00:06:39.733 --> 00:06:43.733
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:49.200
I'm just gonna name them, Daniel Kwan

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:50.795
and Daniel Scheinert,

00:06:50.795 --> 00:06:54.328
(audience applauds)

00:06:54.328 --> 00:06:55.442
Sian Heder,

00:06:55.442 --> 00:06:58.020
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:06:58.020 --> 00:06:59.610
and Chloe Zhao.

00:06:59.610 --> 00:07:03.840
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:07.129
The Labs wouldn't exist without
the artists who participate

00:07:07.129 --> 00:07:09.300
as creative advisors.

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:11.460
There are many in this room tonight,

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:13.290
including Angela Bassett.

00:07:13.290 --> 00:07:15.275
We loved having you at the Lab.

00:07:15.275 --> 00:07:18.358
(audience applauds)

00:07:19.890 --> 00:07:22.110
Their generosity and wisdom shared

00:07:22.110 --> 00:07:24.432
with the next generation
has forever helped

00:07:24.432 --> 00:07:26.887
to shape the visionary work

00:07:26.887 --> 00:07:29.371
and lives of thousands of filmmakers

00:07:29.371 --> 00:07:31.691
from all over the world.

00:07:31.691 --> 00:07:34.620
I'm also most deeply grateful

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:37.078
for the inspiration provided by my family.

00:07:37.078 --> 00:07:40.795
I grew up in a house
surrounded by my father's art

00:07:40.795 --> 00:07:45.795
and was immersed in a world
where creativity knew no bounds.

00:07:46.050 --> 00:07:48.420
Warren Satter's work was everywhere,

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:51.829
serving as a constant reminder
of how art can bring beauty

00:07:51.829 --> 00:07:54.091
and truth into the world.

00:07:54.091 --> 00:07:58.140
Equally impactful was the
influence of my mother,

00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:01.675
Helen Satter, a resilient
holocaust survivor

00:08:01.675 --> 00:08:05.173
who recently celebrated
her hundredth birthday.

00:08:05.173 --> 00:08:08.256
(audience applauds)

00:08:14.130 --> 00:08:16.920
Her unwavering spirit, determination

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:19.140
and compassionate heart taught me

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:23.130
that true fulfillment comes
from using our privileges

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:25.740
and talents to make a positive difference

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:27.453
in the lives of others.

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:32.224
My incredible husband, David Latt,

00:08:32.224 --> 00:08:35.307
(audience applauds)

00:08:37.349 --> 00:08:39.585
has been a constant source of inspiration,

00:08:39.585 --> 00:08:41.893
compassion, and love.

00:08:41.893 --> 00:08:45.300
As a writer and producer,
he helped me understand

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:48.213
what artists need to fulfill
their creative lives.

00:08:48.213 --> 00:08:51.060
And David has always been there for me

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:55.053
sharing our mutual love
of family and giving back.

00:08:56.129 --> 00:08:58.890
We've experienced profound inspiration

00:08:58.890 --> 00:09:01.531
and joy with our son, Franklin Latt.

00:09:01.531 --> 00:09:04.614
(audience applauds)

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:10.410
He's carried on the legacy
of supporting artists

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:13.915
and creatives as his
ongoing work and purpose.

00:09:13.915 --> 00:09:18.240
Through his role as co-head of
Motion Picture Talent at CAA,

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:20.340
he has worked hard to ensure

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:22.475
that artists can thrive in our industry

00:09:22.475 --> 00:09:26.463
and find the opportunities
to do great work.

00:09:28.081 --> 00:09:31.080
I know that many of you are aware

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:34.350
that we lost our younger
son, Michael Latt,

00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:36.074
just a month ago,

00:09:36.074 --> 00:09:39.275
to a tragic act of violence.

00:09:39.275 --> 00:09:42.570
Michael devoted his career
to supporting artists,

00:09:42.570 --> 00:09:45.540
championing organizations
that led elevated

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:46.950
artists of color,

00:09:46.950 --> 00:09:49.024
creating inclusive communities,

00:09:49.024 --> 00:09:54.024
and leveraging storytelling
for enduring social change.

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:57.813
Tonight I share this honor with Michael.

00:09:59.464 --> 00:10:02.547
(audience applauds)

00:10:11.010 --> 00:10:12.840
He will be forever remembered,

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:14.010
living in our hearts

00:10:14.010 --> 00:10:16.310
as someone who led with love,

00:10:16.310 --> 00:10:21.310
always showing up with hope,
compassion, and kindness.

00:10:21.851 --> 00:10:25.328
Nine years ago, the icon Harry Belafonte,

00:10:25.328 --> 00:10:27.720
who is also celebrated with this honor,

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:31.680
shared these beautiful words
which have such meaning for me.

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:35.371
Each and every one of you
has the power, the will,

00:10:35.371 --> 00:10:38.040
the capacity to make a difference

00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:40.590
in the world in which we live.

00:10:40.590 --> 00:10:43.230
Artists are the gatekeepers of truth.

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:45.450
We are civilizations' anchor,

00:10:45.450 --> 00:10:48.301
we're the compass for
humanity's conscience.

00:10:48.301 --> 00:10:51.662
Art in its highest form is art that serves

00:10:51.662 --> 00:10:54.903
and instructs society
and human development.

00:10:57.120 --> 00:10:59.430
Let us take this moment

00:10:59.430 --> 00:11:02.640
to celebrate the collective
impact we can have

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:06.690
when we come together as
an inclusive community.

00:11:06.690 --> 00:11:09.060
And I'm really talking
about all of us here.

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:12.600
Let's stand side by side
to support the artists

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:16.203
who shape our world now
and into the future.

00:11:17.910 --> 00:11:20.550
I hope you'll join me
in this essential work.

00:11:20.550 --> 00:11:22.080
It is essential work,

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:25.504
and find your own special way to give back

00:11:25.504 --> 00:11:28.410
and tell your stories.

00:11:28.410 --> 00:11:29.462
Thank you.

00:11:29.462 --> 00:11:33.102
(audience applauds)

00:11:33.102 --> 00:11:35.602
(theme music)

